Likutay Halachos

Choshen Mishpat &mdash; Volume II

Laws of Unloading and Loading (Perikah u&rsquo;Te&rsquo;inah)

Subdivision of Laws of Lost and Found, Halacha 3, &sect;&sect; 15&ndash;16

Based on Likutay Moharan I:13 &mdash; &ldquo;Ashray ha&rsquo;Am&rdquo; &mdash; Hashgachah Shelaimah (Complete Providence)

&sect; 15

This is the aspect of the mitzvah of perikah u&rsquo;te&rsquo;inah [unloading and loading], the aspect of &ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching beneath its burden, and you would hold back from helping him &mdash; you shall surely help with him&rdquo; (Exodus 23:5).

For it is explained in the Torah [discourse] mentioned above that the tzadik, through his great power, elevates and raises up through the aspect of ibbur [spiritual gestation] even those souls that are very distant &mdash; those who have not yet left from the profane to the holy even a hairsbreadth. This is the aspect of &ldquo;Go out and see the might of your Master&rdquo; etc. [i.e. that even the lowest depths are reached], see there.

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching beneath its burden.&rdquo; And our Sages of blessed memory expounded: &ldquo;one who hates you&rdquo; &mdash; this refers to one whom you saw transgress a sin, etc. And this is &ldquo;crouching beneath its burden&rdquo; &mdash; meaning that it is difficult to elevate him and raise him up because of the greatness of the burden placed upon him, which is the aspect of the burden of sins, in the aspect of &ldquo;as a heavy burden, they are too heavy for me&rdquo; (Psalms 38:5). And therefore it could be that it would enter your mind to hold back from helping him, in the aspect of &ldquo;and you would hold back from helping him,&rdquo; for it would seem to you that it is too difficult to raise him because of the greatness of the burden upon him. But Hashem, may He be blessed, is merciful and gracious, and His compassion is exceedingly great. Therefore He warned in His mercy: &ldquo;you shall surely help with him&rdquo; &mdash; that even such a soul, one must help and endeavor with all one&rsquo;s strength to elevate and raise it up, for the tzadik has such power, as mentioned above.

And this is &ldquo;with him&rdquo; precisely, as our Sages of blessed memory expounded: &ldquo;One might think even if he [the owner] sits down and says, &lsquo;since you have a mitzvah [to help]&rsquo;&rdquo; etc.? &ldquo;Therefore it says: &lsquo;with him.&rsquo;&rdquo; That is to say, certainly it is a great mitzvah to occupy oneself with him and to help him also to elevate and renew his soul. However, all this is &ldquo;with him&rdquo; &mdash; meaning, when he himself also wants to engage in this, to have compassion on himself, to endeavor in the rectification of his soul, to remove the great burden that is upon him, only that he cannot do so by himself and he is waiting for someone who will help him in this. Then it is a great mitzvah to help him with all one&rsquo;s strength, to occupy oneself with him, to raise him up. For there is the power to renew his soul as well, if he himself has some genuine desire for this, to engage in the rectification of his soul. But if he wants to exempt himself entirely and does not want to endeavor in rectifying his soul at all, but rather wants the tzadik himself to do everything for him while he does nothing at all and does not engage in this at all &mdash; then certainly the tzadik need not occupy himself with him, for it is impossible to help him when he does not want to engage in his own rectification at all, as mentioned above.

And as our Master, our Teacher and our Rebbe, of blessed memory, said to someone: &ldquo;You without me cannot do anything at all&rdquo; (i.e. to rectify his soul by himself), &ldquo;but I too without you cannot do a thing&rdquo; &mdash; meaning, if you yourself do nothing at all, certainly it is impossible for me to do everything for you. For a person has free will, and it is impossible to raise him up unless he has some arousal from himself. And this is: &ldquo;One might think even if he sits down&rdquo; etc.? &ldquo;Therefore it says: &lsquo;with him,&rsquo;&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above.

&sect; 16

This is also the aspect of the mitzvah of perikah u&rsquo;te&rsquo;inah in its simple, literal sense. For the fact that one&rsquo;s donkey crouches beneath its burden and one has such distress and weariness on the road &mdash; this derives from the aspect of &ldquo;by the sweat of your brow shall you eat bread&rdquo; (Genesis 3:19), the aspect of &ldquo;in sorrow shall you eat of it&rdquo; (Genesis 3:17). For from there come all the suffering and weariness that a person has in the pursuits he engages in for his livelihood. And it is impossible to rectify this and be saved from it except through the rectifications stated in the Torah [discourse] mentioned above &mdash; namely, through breaking the desire for money by means of tzedakah. Through this, one merits to nullify the aspect of &ldquo;by the sweat of your brow,&rdquo; etc., and through this one merits da&rsquo;as [holy knowledge]. Through this, one can gather nefashos [souls] and draw forth Torah, etc. Through this, hashgachah shelaimah [complete providence] is drawn down, as explained there.

And when hashgachah shelaimah is drawn down, then everything is rectified in completeness. For all the suffering and distress and weariness, etc. &mdash; all of it is because one is distant from Hashem, may He be blessed, namely from the Torah. Through this distance, one does not merit hashgachah shelaimah, and from there come all the lack of livelihood and the withholding of shefa [abundance], and all the suffering and weariness that one has in matters of livelihood. For all livelihood and shefa flow from the eyes of His providence, may He be blessed. But when one is distant, G&ndash;d forbid, from the Torah, so that the hashgachah does not reach him in completeness, etc., as explained there &mdash; then the shefa that flows from His providence, may He be blessed, is scattered, G&ndash;d forbid, and goes to the place where it goes, G&ndash;d forbid, and does not reach him. And from this comes all the difficulty of livelihood, may the Merciful One save us. For because one is distant from the Torah, G&ndash;d forbid, and distant from complete hashgachah, the shefa flows to the place that it flows to, as mentioned above &mdash; namely, to the outer [impure] places, and the nations of the world and the great wicked ones who are connected there receive all the shefa, while Israel is sustained from the dregs. For Israel clings to Hashem, may He be blessed, and they need to receive the shefa specifically from the aspect of hashgachah shelaimah.

And this is what is written regarding the Land of Israel: &ldquo;Constantly, the eyes of Hashem your G&ndash;d are upon it&rdquo; (Deuteronomy 11:12). &ldquo;Constantly&rdquo; precisely &mdash; this is the aspect of hashgachah shelaimah, that His eyes, may He be blessed, gaze constantly upon the Land of Israel. For the eyes of His providence reach there in the aspect of a striking [of the gaze] until it returns and is depicted in His eyes, and therefore His eyes are attached there constantly. For the hashgachah shelaimah is in the aspect of &ldquo;and the chayos were running and returning&rdquo; (Ezekiel 1:14), as explained there &mdash; meaning that at every moment the hashgachah runs and returns from above to below and returns from below to above, and so it cycles continuously at every moment in the aspect of &ldquo;running and returning.&rdquo; This is the aspect of hashgachah shelaimah, the aspect of the Land of Israel, the aspect of &ldquo;constantly the eyes of Hashem your G&ndash;d are upon it&rdquo; &mdash; &ldquo;constantly&rdquo; precisely. For the Land of Israel is near to Him, may He be blessed, more than anywhere in the world, for there is the essential drawing of the Torah, as is known.

And therefore, one whose donkey crouches beneath its burden and has such weariness &mdash; which derives from the blemish of the desire for money, from the aspect of &ldquo;by the sweat of your brow,&rdquo; etc., from the aspect of the blemish of hashgachah shelaimah, whose essential rectification comes through returning and renewing his soul through the aspect of ibbur, through which hashgachah shelaimah is once again drawn upon him, as mentioned above &mdash; therefore it might enter your mind that it is impossible to help such a person, since he has fallen so far. And therefore the Torah warned: &ldquo;You shall surely help with him&rdquo; &mdash; for in truth one must share in his suffering and help him with all one&rsquo;s strength. And through helping him physically and fulfilling the mitzvah &ldquo;you shall surely help&rdquo; and &ldquo;you shall surely raise up,&rdquo; through this kindness that one does with him, one subdues and nullifies from him the aspect of &ldquo;by the sweat of your brow,&rdquo; etc. &mdash; which is the aspect of the blemish of the desire for money &mdash; and one draws upon him the aspect of hashgachah shelaimah.

For upon each and every one of Israel this matter is incumbent: to endeavor in rectifying one&rsquo;s fellow both physically and spiritually, and to fulfill with one&rsquo;s fellow &ldquo;you shall surely help&rdquo; and &ldquo;you shall surely raise up.&rdquo; For even though one&rsquo;s fellow is very distant from Hashem, may He be blessed, nevertheless one can help him too through the power of the true tzadik, who has the power to elevate even his soul in the aspect of &ldquo;Go out and see the might of your Master,&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above. See there well what is brought there: &ldquo;And the wave cries out to its fellow: &lsquo;Is there anything in the world?&rsquo;&rdquo; etc., &ldquo;Go out and see the might of your Master,&rdquo; etc. See there. It emerges that each and every one must arouse and remind his fellow in yiras shamayim [the fear of Heaven], even though they are as they are. Nevertheless, through the power of the tzadik, they can all ascend, as mentioned above. And therefore the Torah warned to help even the sonay [the &ldquo;enemy&rdquo;] &mdash; i.e. one whom you saw transgress a sin &mdash; for even him one must help greatly, for there is the power to elevate him as well through the power of the great tzadik who is engaged in this, in the aspect of &ldquo;Go out and see the might of your Master,&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above.

&bull; &bull; &bull;

Laws of Unloading and Loading &mdash; Halacha 1

Based on Likutay Moharan I:59 &mdash; &ldquo;Mi She&rsquo;Osaik L&rsquo;Karaiv&rdquo; &mdash; Whoever engages in drawing close [to Hashem those who are far]

&ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching beneath its burden&rdquo; etc. &ldquo;you shall surely help with him,&rdquo; and the Targum renders: &ldquo;You shall abandon what is in your heart against him, and you shall unload with him.&rdquo;

&sect; 1

For the heaviness of the burden that becomes heavy and falls &mdash; this is through sinah [hatred]. For hatred is a blemish of da&rsquo;as [knowledge/mind], as Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote many times, as mentioned above. And when da&rsquo;as is blemished, then the burden becomes heavy and falls, for da&rsquo;as raises all things, in the aspect of &ldquo;the living carries itself&rdquo; (Talmud). And the essential life-force is through da&rsquo;as, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 7:12): &ldquo;Wisdom gives life.&rdquo; And therefore, through da&rsquo;as, a person can raise all the burdens in the world with ease, through stratagems and counsel. But when da&rsquo;as departs, the life-force departs, and then the burden falls.

And this is the aspect of the choli nofel [falling sickness, i.e. epilepsy] that Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote about in the discourse &ldquo;Hai man d&rsquo;ba&rsquo;ay l&rsquo;minsav&rdquo; (LM I:29). See there, that it comes about through a blemish of da&rsquo;as, through the overpowering of the bloods, and through this one falls. See there. And hatred and anger are the aspect of overpowering of the bloods, as Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote (LM I:57): for &ldquo;the liver is angry&rdquo; (Berachos 61a), and the liver is full of blood, which is the aspect of hatred and murder, which is the spilling of blood. And therefore, through hatred, there comes the overpowering of the bloods, and through this da&rsquo;as is blemished, and through this one falls in the aspect of choli nofel, as mentioned above. For then the burden becomes heavy, in the aspect of &ldquo;for my sins have gone over my head; as a heavy burden they are too heavy for me&rdquo; (Psalms 38:5). For through sins, which are the aspect of the overpowering of the bloods, as Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote there &mdash; through this da&rsquo;as is blemished, and then all things fall through the heaviness, in the aspect of &ldquo;as a heavy burden, they are too heavy for me.&rdquo; See the discourse Ha&rsquo;i Man mentioned above, where Rabbainu, may his light shine, brought this very verse. See there well.

And this is what is written: &ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching&rdquo; etc. &mdash; &ldquo;one who hates you&rdquo; precisely, for the essential falling of the burden is through hatred, as mentioned above. And therefore the Torah commanded to help one&rsquo;s fellow to unload and load, in order that there should be love and affection between them. And through this, the blemish of the falling of the burden &mdash; which comes through hatred &mdash; is rectified, as mentioned above. And this is the meaning of the Targum: &ldquo;You shall abandon what is in your heart&rdquo; etc. For the essential purpose of this mitzvah is for the sake of love, as mentioned above. And as our Sages of blessed memory said regarding this mitzvah, on the verse &ldquo;You have established equity&rdquo; (Psalms 99:4) &mdash; that one who sees his enemy unloading and loading with him will certainly embrace him and kiss him. For the essential purpose of this mitzvah is for the sake of love, through which the blemish of the falling of the burden is rectified, which comes through hatred, which is a blemish of da&rsquo;as, as mentioned above.

&sect; 2

And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;If he is an elder and it is not in accordance with his honor, he is exempt&rdquo; (Bava Metzia 30a). For one must be careful not to blemish the kavod [honor/glory]. For kavod is the root of the nefesh [soul], as mentioned above. It follows that it is the life-force of the soul, and it is in the aspect of mochin [expanded consciousness] and life-force for the soul. For kavod depends on da&rsquo;as, for &ldquo;it is not fitting for a fool to have honor&rdquo; (Proverbs 26:1). And as it is written (Proverbs 3:35): &ldquo;The wise shall inherit honor.&rdquo; And as Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote (LM I:67) that kavod returns to those who perceive knowledge, for there is the root of kavod. And therefore a blemish of kavod is a blemish of da&rsquo;as.

Also, love and unity depend on kavod, for there all the souls are unified, in the aspect of &ldquo;in their assembly let my honor not be united&rdquo; (Genesis 49:6) &mdash; for kavod is in the aspect of unity, for kavod is the aspect of Malchus [Kingship], which is the aspect of unity, as brought above. For the king restrains the nation and gathers them and unifies them, as it is written (I Samuel 9:17): &ldquo;This one shall restrain My people.&rdquo; And similarly, it is found in the words of Rabbainu, may his light shine (LM I:14), that through rectifying kavod &mdash; namely through giving honor to those who fear Hashem &mdash; through this, peace is drawn into the world. See there.

It emerges that the rectification of all the souls and their life-force is the aspect of kavod, as mentioned above. And when kavod is blemished, then their mochin and life-force are blemished, and then they fall, as mentioned above. For they become in the aspect of a masa kavaid [heavy burden], as mentioned above, which is the aspect of a blemish of kavod. For the blemish of kavod is that the Vav &mdash; which is the aspect of life-force &mdash; departs, and then from kavod [כָּבוֹד, honor] it becomes kavaid [כָּבֵד, heavy], the aspect of &ldquo;a heavy burden&rdquo; &mdash; as Rabbainu wrote in another place, in the discourse &ldquo;Ani Hashem Hu Sh&rsquo;mi&rdquo; (LM I:11).

And therefore, if he is an elder and it is not in accordance with his honor, he is exempt from perikah u&rsquo;te&rsquo;inah &mdash; and there is an opinion that it is forbidden &mdash; for the essential blemish of the falling of the burden is in the aspect of the blemish of kavod, as mentioned above. And therefore one must be careful with one&rsquo;s honor so as not to blemish it through the falling of the burden. For on the contrary, now one must be extremely careful with one&rsquo;s honor, which is the aspect of rectifying the falling, as mentioned above. And the essential rectification of kavod is through the honor of the elder, as found in the words of Rabbainu, may his light shine, many times.

And therefore the elder, who merits completeness of da&rsquo;as, in the aspect of &ldquo;this one has acquired wisdom,&rdquo; and merits kavod, in the aspect of &ldquo;the wise shall inherit honor,&rdquo; as it is written (Job 15:10): &ldquo;and with the elders is honor&rdquo; &mdash; therefore he can bear and raise all the burdens, as Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote (LM I:54) that the tzadik is called masa [burden-bearer], for he bears everything, in the aspect of &ldquo;as a nurse carries the infant&rdquo; (Numbers 11:12). For the tzadik lifts and raises all the fallings. And all this is through rectifying da&rsquo;as, as mentioned above, and the rectification of da&rsquo;as is the rectification of kavod, as mentioned above, and as is implied from the words of Rabbainu, may his light shine, in many places.

&sect; 3

And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said regarding this verse &mdash; that the sonay [enemy] spoken of here is one whom you saw transgress a sin. This is the aspect of the overpowering of the bloods of the 365 sinews, which correspond to the 365 negative commandments, as Rabbainu, of blessed memory, wrote there. And from this very thing comes the aspect of the falling, in the aspect of &ldquo;for my sins have gone over my head; as a heavy burden&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above. For it is forbidden to hate a fellow Jew unless one saw him transgress a sin, in which case it is a mitzvah to hate him, as our Sages of blessed memory said regarding this matter. For the sin comes through the overpowering of the bloods of the 365 sinews, as mentioned above. And from there comes the aspect of hatred &mdash; through the bloods, as mentioned above. And therefore one is permitted to hate him, for he is in the aspect of hatred, as mentioned above.

And nevertheless the Torah commanded not to leave him to fall completely, but rather one must abandon what is in one&rsquo;s heart against him and turn his [situation] favorably, to bring him to the aspect of love, as the Targum renders: &ldquo;You shall abandon&rdquo; etc. And through this, one raises him from his falling, as mentioned above.

&sect; 4

And now, let us hint at this mitzvah as applied to a person himself. &ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching,&rdquo; etc. &mdash; when one sees the chomro [his materiality, his body; a wordplay on chamor, donkey] of one who has sinned, as mentioned above, crouching beneath its burden and unable to bear upon itself the burden and yoke of Torah, and he falls, G&ndash;d forbid &mdash; for it is known that a person must be &ldquo;as an ox to the yoke and as a donkey to the burden&rdquo; &mdash; and when one sees a person falling from the service of Hashem, G&ndash;d forbid, and this is through sins, G&ndash;d forbid, the aspect of son&rsquo;acha [one who hates you], as mentioned above &mdash; the Torah commanded not to hide oneself from him, but rather &ldquo;you shall surely help with him&rdquo;: one must see to help him and draw him close and raise him from his falling. And one must love him specifically, as the Targum renders &ldquo;you shall abandon&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above.

For one must judge every person favorably. And even in a completely wicked person, one must find some merit. And through finding some point of good in him, one takes him out of his wickedness and brings him genuinely into the scale of merit, and he returns in teshuvah [repentance]. As Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote (LM I:282) on the verse &ldquo;Yet a little while and the wicked is no more&rdquo; etc. (Psalms 37:10). See there. And this is the mitzvah of &ldquo;you shall surely help&rdquo; &mdash; for the essential purpose of the mitzvah is love, as the Targum renders, etc., as mentioned above. For also to raise a person himself from his falling is only through love &mdash; namely, through bringing him into the scale of merit and love, as mentioned above.

And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;An elder and it is not in accordance with his honor &mdash; he is exempt, and some say it is forbidden.&rdquo; For when one engages in drawing people close to the service of Hashem, may He be blessed, to raise them from their fallings, one must guard one&rsquo;s honor very carefully &mdash; namely, that one should not draw close those who are not fit to be drawn close, for through them one&rsquo;s honor would be blemished, G&ndash;d forbid. As it is written regarding Moshe: &ldquo;Go down&rdquo; (Exodus 32:7), etc. &mdash; because he drew close the airev rav [mixed multitude]. For these wicked ones are in the aspect of the airev rav, who are the aspect of Aisav, as is brought. And of them it is said (Obadiah 1:2): &ldquo;You are greatly despised&rdquo; &mdash; for they have no share in the kavod of holiness. And therefore it is forbidden to draw them close, for one&rsquo;s honor would be blemished, G&ndash;d forbid, through them.

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;an elder and it is not in accordance with his honor&rdquo; &mdash; that it is forbidden to raise him from his falling when one sees that it is not in accordance with one&rsquo;s honor, meaning that one&rsquo;s honor would be blemished through this, G&ndash;d forbid, as mentioned above. For the essential thing is kavod. For when one draws close to Hashem, may He be blessed, those who are fit to be drawn close, His honor, may He be blessed, is magnified. For this is the essential honor: when those who are far away draw close to His service, may He be blessed, as Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote all this. And they become the aspect of the Haichal HaKodesh [Holy Temple], in the aspect of &ldquo;and in His Temple everything says: &lsquo;Honor!&rsquo;&rdquo; (Psalms 29:9). And this is the aspect of the nullification of the forbidden in sixty &mdash; the aspect of &ldquo;sixty are the queens&rdquo; (Song of Songs 6:8), the aspect of the King of Honor, the aspect of the Haichal HaKodesh mentioned above, through which the prohibition and evil within them is nullified. See all this in the discourse &ldquo;Mi She&rsquo;Osaik L&rsquo;Karaiv&rdquo; etc. (LM I:59).

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;you shall surely help&rdquo; &mdash; meaning, to support him and raise him from his falling. This is the aspect of the nullification of the forbidden in Samech [ס, the letter whose numerical value is 60], as mentioned above, in the aspect of &ldquo;Hashem supports all who fall&rdquo; (Psalms 145:14). As our Sages of blessed memory said (Berachos 4b): &ldquo;Why is there no Nun in Ashray? Because it contains falling&rdquo; etc. &ldquo;Yet even so, David returned and supported it through ruach ha&rsquo;kodesh, as it is written: &lsquo;Hashem supports all who fall.&rsquo;&rdquo; It emerges that Samech is the aspect of supporting the falling, in the aspect of &ldquo;She has fallen; she shall not rise again &mdash; the maiden of Israel&rdquo; (Amos 5:2). And this is also the nullification of all prohibitions through [the letter] Samech, for all prohibitions come from the aspect of fallen things, as explained above in the Laws of Mixtures. See there.

It emerges that the essential mitzvah of &ldquo;you shall surely help&rdquo; &mdash; meaning, to support him and raise him from his falling &mdash; is through the aspect of kavod, which is the aspect of Samech, the aspect of &ldquo;sixty are the queens,&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above. And from there comes the support for all who fall, in the aspect of &ldquo;Hashem supports,&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above. And therefore those who are remote from kavod and no longer have any hold in it &mdash; it is forbidden to draw them close, for the essential thing is kavod, which is the root of all the souls of holiness, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;an elder and it is not in accordance with his honor,&rdquo; as mentioned above.

And therefore there are two opinions: according to one opinion, he is exempt, but if he wants [to help], it is permitted; and according to the second opinion, it is forbidden. And each master stated one thing and the other master stated another, and they do not dispute one another. For it all depends on the degree of their wickedness and the extent of their distance from kavod. For some are so far away that there is great danger in drawing them close, and because of this the Torah exempted the elder, for he is not obligated to endanger himself in such great danger. Nevertheless, if he wishes to go beyond the letter of the law and descend to raise even these &mdash; he certainly performs an exceedingly great mitzvah. But some are even more distant, to the point that there is an absolute prohibition to draw them close, and it is forbidden for him to endanger himself on their account, as found in the discourse mentioned above regarding the statement of our Sages of blessed memory: &ldquo;Why are the eyes of the Tarmudians bleary?&rdquo; etc. (LM I:59). But those who still have some hold in kavod, even though they are very far &mdash; regarding them it is said &ldquo;you shall surely help,&rdquo; as mentioned above, for this is the essential honor, as mentioned above.

Blessed is Hashem forever, Amen and Amen.

Laws of Unloading and Loading &mdash; Halacha 2

Based on Likutay Moharan II:7 &mdash; &ldquo;Ki M&rsquo;Rachamam Y&rsquo;nahagaim&rdquo; &mdash; For the One Who has mercy on them shall lead them

&ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching beneath its burden, and you would hold back&rdquo; etc. &ldquo;you shall surely help with him.&rdquo;

For it is found in the discourse &ldquo;Vay&rsquo;hi Mi'Kaitz &mdash; Ki M&rsquo;Rachamam Y&rsquo;nahagaim&rdquo; etc. in Likutay Tinyana (LM II:7), that the essential rachamanus [compassion] is when one of Israel falls into sins, G&ndash;d forbid. For this is a heavy burden that it is impossible for a Jew to bear at all &mdash; such a heavy burden, etc. See there. And one must have compassion on Israel, to extract them from sins and to instill da&rsquo;as in them, etc. For one must illuminate the da&rsquo;as in children and students, etc. See there. And through the combined aspect of &ldquo;child and student,&rdquo; from there the parnasah [livelihood] is drawn, which the Malchus receives from the &ldquo;sea of wisdom,&rdquo; etc. See there all this well.

And this is hinted at in the mitzvah mentioned above. For sonay [enemy], our Sages of blessed memory explained, refers to one whom you saw transgress a sin. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;crouching beneath its burden&rdquo; &mdash; for the heaviness of the burden overpowers him, which is the burden of sin. And because of this he crouches beneath his burden, for it is impossible for him to bear at all such a burden, as mentioned above. And this is &ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching,&rdquo; etc. For the overpowering of sin is in the aspect of chomer [materiality] and the animal spirit. For when the burden of sin overpowers, G&ndash;d forbid, one is not called by the name adam [human], for the essential humanity is da&rsquo;as. Rather, one is in the aspect of an animal in human form, as explained in the discourse mentioned above. See there.

And the Torah warned each person who sees his fellow upon whom the burden of sin has overpowered, G&ndash;d forbid &mdash; for only this is truly called a masa [burden], as mentioned above &mdash; that it is forbidden to hide one&rsquo;s eyes, but rather one is obligated to have compassion on one&rsquo;s fellow and to help him, to fulfill &ldquo;you shall surely help with him.&rdquo; For it is incumbent upon each and every Jew to have compassion on one&rsquo;s fellow with the true compassion, which is greater than every other kind of compassion &mdash; namely, to help him unload from upon himself the heavy burden of sin, which it is impossible for him to bear at all, such a heavy burden, as mentioned above. For every person is obligated to endeavor to look after his fellow, to speak with him about yiras shamayim [fear of Heaven], to extract him from the heavy burden of sins, which is heavier than all other burdens. For this is the essential compassion, as mentioned above. And this is &ldquo;you shall surely help with him.&rdquo;

And also the mitzvah itself in its literal sense &mdash; namely, actual perikah u&rsquo;te&rsquo;inah, to help one&rsquo;s fellow actually unload the burden from upon his donkey &mdash; is also in this very aspect. For the burden upon the animal, which is his money and livelihood, is drawn from the &ldquo;sea of wisdom,&rdquo; which comes through the combined aspect of &ldquo;child and student&rdquo; &mdash; namely through illuminating da&rsquo;as in child and student, in proportion to how much one extracts them from the heavy burden of sin. This connection is well understood in the discourse mentioned above. See there.

And therefore one who merits to emerge from the heavy burden of sins and merits da&rsquo;as &mdash; which is the aspect of illuminating &ldquo;child and student&rdquo; &mdash; similarly, his livelihood comes to him easily. For from there is the essential source of livelihood, as mentioned above. And conversely, G&ndash;d forbid, one upon whom the burden of sin overpowers, G&ndash;d forbid, and who lacks da&rsquo;as, G&ndash;d forbid &mdash; then his livelihood comes with great heaviness and immense weariness, in the aspect of &ldquo;the people went about and gathered&rdquo; (Numbers 11:8) &mdash; &ldquo;in foolishness, they ground it in mills or crushed it in a mortar.&rdquo; This is the aspect of the heaviness of livelihood for one who has not driven out the spirit of foolishness, as found in the holy Zohar, which hints at the heaviness of livelihood for one who lacks da&rsquo;as, as mentioned above.

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;A person&rsquo;s sustenance is as difficult as the splitting of the Sea of Reeds&rdquo; (Pesachim 118a). For the splitting of the Sea of Reeds alludes to the &ldquo;sea of wisdom&rdquo; mentioned above. For this is the essential aspect of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds: that the sea of wisdom was split open and revealed, as is brought. This is the aspect of the &ldquo;sea of wisdom&rdquo; mentioned above that is revealed in proportion to how much one merits to emerge from sins, as mentioned above. And from there livelihood is drawn, as mentioned above. And this is &ldquo;sustenance is as difficult as the splitting of the Sea of Reeds&rdquo; &mdash; for specifically from there is sustenance and livelihood drawn, from the aspect of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, from the aspect of the &ldquo;sea of wisdom&rdquo; mentioned above. For the essential difficulty of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds was because of sins, in the aspect of &ldquo;these are idolaters and these are idolaters,&rdquo; as our Sages of blessed memory said. And similarly, sustenance is difficult, for in proportion to how much one merits to emerge from sins, so one merits that the sea of wisdom is revealed &mdash; which is the aspect of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds &mdash; and so one merits sustenance and livelihood, for from there livelihood is drawn, as mentioned above.

It emerges that the essential heaviness and yoke of livelihood is because of the heaviness of the burden of sins. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching beneath its burden&rdquo; &mdash; that the burden of his money and livelihood is heavy upon him, and he has suffering and great heaviness. And this derives only from the heavy burden of sins, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;one who hates you&rdquo; &mdash; for sonay [enemy] is none other than one who transgressed a sin, as our Sages of blessed memory explained. And the Torah warned not to hold back from helping him, but rather one must abandon from the heart what one holds against him and help him, as the Targum renders: &ldquo;You shall abandon what is in your heart against him, and you shall unload with him.&rdquo;

And at first glance the matter is puzzling. For granted, if the meaning of sonay were the simple sense &mdash; that he hates him on his own account, because of a quarrel or dispute or the like &mdash; it would be fitting to say &ldquo;you shall abandon,&rdquo; etc., that one must forgive him. But since our Sages of blessed memory explained that the sonay stated in the Torah is because one saw him transgress a sin, how is it fitting to say &ldquo;you shall abandon what is in your heart against him&rdquo;? Yet in truth, based on what is explained in the words of Rabbainu, of blessed memory, in many places &mdash; that one must judge every person favorably, and one must find merit even in a completely wicked person, for how is it possible that he has not done some small amount of good in his days? And through finding some good point in him, one genuinely brings him into the scale of merit, and it is possible to return him in teshuvah through this, as brought on the verse &ldquo;Yet a little while and the wicked is no more&rdquo; etc. See there (LM I:282).

And this is the matter mentioned above: that even though he is son&rsquo;acha &mdash; that you saw him transgress a sin &mdash; nevertheless, do not refrain from helping him. On the contrary: &ldquo;you shall abandon what is in your heart against him,&rdquo; for you must find merit in him and have compassion on him and forgive him. For through finding merit in him, one genuinely brings him into the scale of merit, and one returns him in teshuvah. It emerges that it is in the power of each Jew to forgive the sins of his fellow, for he can bring him into the scale of merit by finding some merit in him, as mentioned above. And therefore, when one sees that his fellow has suffering on the road, that his donkey crouches beneath its burden &mdash; and the heaviness of this burden is because of the heaviness of the burden of sin, as mentioned above &mdash; one is obligated to have compassion on him and forgive him, namely, to find merit in him and bring him into the scale of merit, as mentioned above, which is the essential compassion. And to help him unload the burden. And through abandoning the hatred and having compassion on him and instilling compassion within him, because one seeks and finds some merit in him &mdash; through this one genuinely brings him into the scale of merit and unloads from upon him the heavy burden of sins. And through this very act, one unloads from upon him the actual [physical] burden, for the essential heaviness of the burden is because of the burden of sin, as mentioned above.

For in truth, the essential purpose of this mitzvah of perikah u&rsquo;te&rsquo;inah is for the sake of love and compassion, as our Sages of blessed memory said on the verse &ldquo;You have established equity&rdquo; (Psalms 99:4): &ldquo;Do you have an enemy who sees&rdquo; etc. For the essential purpose of the mitzvah is to unload the burden of sin, and this is accomplished through love and compassion, as mentioned above.

Blessed is Hashem forever, Amen.

[The following halacha, Perikah u&rsquo;Te&rsquo;inah 3 (corresponding to Avaidah u&rsquo;Metziah 3, &sect;15), is a cross-reference and has been included above in the subdivision.]

Laws of Unloading and Loading &mdash; Halacha 4

Based on Likutay Moharan II:2 &mdash; &ldquo;Y&rsquo;may Chanukah Haim Y&rsquo;may Hoda&rsquo;ah&rdquo; &mdash; The days of Chanukah are days of thanksgiving (&sect;5)

&sect; 1

One must draw the holiness of Shabbos into the six days of the week. For Shabbos is the aspect of a &ldquo;son,&rdquo; the aspect of rest for those above and those below, etc. And the weekdays are the aspect of a &ldquo;servant&rdquo; and sadness, etc. And one must draw the rest and joy of Shabbos into the weekdays, etc. And through this, the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut [simple unity] of the Blessed One is revealed &mdash; that all the pe&rsquo;ulos mishtanos [diverse activities/phenomena] flow from the One Who is Simple, may He be blessed. This is impossible to understand through human intellect, but only through emunah [faith] that is drawn through Shabbos &mdash; through drawing the holiness and joy of Shabbos into the weekdays, etc.

And through this, the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut, may He be blessed, is revealed &mdash; that all the diverse activities flow from the One Who is Simple, may He be blessed, which is impossible to understand through human intellect, but only through emunah drawn through Shabbos, through drawing the holiness and joy of Shabbos into the six weekdays, etc. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;an individual and a majority &mdash; the halachah follows the majority.&rdquo; For when the many agree to the view of one &mdash; this is the aspect of diverse activities being included in the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut. And this is very precious before Hashem, may He be blessed, etc. See there.

&sect; 2

And this is the aspect of the great emphasis placed on the mitzvah of &ldquo;you shall surely help&rdquo; &mdash; that one should abandon the hatred in one&rsquo;s heart toward one&rsquo;s fellow and unload with him, as it is written: &ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching beneath its burden, and you would hold back from helping him &mdash; you shall surely help with him,&rdquo; and the Targum renders: &ldquo;You shall abandon what is in your heart against him, and you shall unload with him.&rdquo; And similarly our Sages of blessed memory said in the Gemara that this is the essential purpose of the mitzvah of perikah u&rsquo;te&rsquo;inah.

For all the suffering that a person has from the heaviness of burdens &mdash; all of it derives from a blemish of the aspect mentioned above, namely, that one did not merit to properly connect all the diverse activities to the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut, because one did not draw the holiness of Shabbos into the six weekdays, as mentioned above. For all the suffering that a person has, G&ndash;d forbid, in body, soul, and money, may the Merciful One save us &mdash; all of them are the aspect of burdens. For suffering is against one&rsquo;s will, and they are like a heavy burden upon him. And all of them come from the aspect of diverse activities when one does not endeavor to reveal and illuminate within them the aspect of the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut. Then the multiplicity of changes intensifies and things do not proceed in the proper order fitting for them from their root, and from there come all the heaviness of burdens and all the suffering, G&ndash;d forbid.

For all suffering, G&ndash;d forbid, is the aspect of machlokes [strife/conflict], for it derives from the conflict of the elements with one another. And it is against the person&rsquo;s will, for the person wants things to be thus, according to his will, that they should proceed in orderly fashion, but they stand against him and dispute with him and proceed not according to his will. And the greater the change against his will, the greater the suffering, G&ndash;d forbid. And all this derives from the blemish of the aspect mentioned above &mdash; that one did not endeavor to draw the joy and rest of Shabbos into the weekdays, and did not draw the revelation of the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut from within the diverse activities.

For according to the order by which the life-force flows from the One Who is Simple to all the diverse activities, everything should proceed in the proper order. For from Him, may He be blessed, no good is withheld, and He in His mercy, may He be blessed, created His world for good &mdash; that the life-force should flow from His simple unity to all the diverse activities, to each and every activity as is fitting, in the proper order, for good. Only because everything was created for the sake of man&rsquo;s free will &mdash; that according to one&rsquo;s complete faith and according to one&rsquo;s deeds, so one draws the life-force from the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut to all the diverse activities, for good, or to the contrary, G&ndash;d forbid. And therefore the essential rectification is through Shabbos, when there is rest and cessation. Through Shabbos, through drawing the holiness of Shabbos into the weekdays, through this the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut is revealed from within the diverse activities, and then everything proceeds in the proper order. For from Shabbos the aspect of rest and cessation is drawn even to all the diverse activities that are done during the weekdays &mdash; to sweeten and nullify the aspect of sadness and the harsh servitude of uvdin d&rsquo;chol [weekday occupations], so that all of one&rsquo;s affairs and endeavors proceed in the proper order, without weariness and harsh labor.

But each person, according to the extent of his blemish in this &mdash; that he does not endeavor to draw the holiness of Shabbos into the weekdays and does not reveal the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut from within the diverse activities &mdash; so the multiplicity of changes of the diverse activities intensifies and they do not proceed in the proper order, and from there come all the suffering, G&ndash;d forbid, which are the aspect of burdens, which are the aspect of machlokes, as mentioned above.

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;When you see the donkey of one who hates you crouching beneath its burden&rdquo; &mdash; that the burden is heavy upon him and the person has suffering from this. All of this derives from the blemish mentioned above, which is the blemish of machlokes, which is the aspect of the blemish of Shabbos, etc. For regarding Shabbos it is written: &ldquo;So that your ox and your donkey may rest, like you&rdquo; (Exodus 23:12). And our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;Give it rest.&rdquo; And from there, rest is drawn upon the ox and the donkey and the like also during the weekdays &mdash; so that even when work is done with them, the labor should not be excessively heavy upon them, as explained above, that one must draw the holiness of Shabbos into the weekdays, as mentioned above. And therefore, when the animal crouches beneath its burden, this derives from the aspect of the blemish of Shabbos, which is the aspect of the blemish of machlokes, etc., as mentioned above.

Therefore the verse warned that one should abandon the hatred in one&rsquo;s heart toward one&rsquo;s fellow, as it is written: &ldquo;you shall surely help &mdash; you shall abandon what is in your heart against him, and you shall unload with him.&rdquo; For the essential abandoning and helping that one helps him regarding the burden is through abandoning the hatred and seeking love &mdash; namely, through the nullification of machlokes and the difference of opinions. Through this, all are included as one in the aspect of the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut, as explained above: that the more Israel is included together in greater love and friendship, the more the aspect of the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut is revealed from within the diverse activities, which is the aspect of the holiness of Shabbos. Through this, all the heaviness of burdens and all the suffering are nullified, for rest is drawn upon everything, as mentioned above.

Ownerless Property and the Convert&rsquo;s Estate (Hefker v&rsquo;Nichsay HaGer)

&sect;&sect; 3&ndash;4, continuing from Perikah u&rsquo;Te&rsquo;inah 4

&sect; 3

This is the aspect of &ldquo;whoever is first in the estate of a convert acquires it&rdquo; (Bava Basra 54a). For the convert who converts &mdash; this is the aspect of the diverse activities that are very remote being included within the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut. For the convert, who was initially a gentile from among the seventy nations, is the essential aspect of pe&rsquo;ulos mishtanos [diverse activities]. For the holy nation of Israel is called &ldquo;one nation in the land&rdquo; (II Samuel 7:23), and therefore they are in the aspect of the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut, as opposed to the seventy nations. For &ldquo;the Torah and the Holy One, blessed is He, and Israel are all one,&rdquo; and therefore the seventy souls of the house of Yaakov are called one nefesh [soul], while the six souls of Aisav are called nefashos [souls] in the plural, as Rashi explained there &mdash; for they are far from unity and simplicity, because they did not receive the Torah. And therefore they are the essential aspect of the diverse activities.

And when one of them comes and converts, this is the aspect of the very remote diverse activities being included within the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut. For initially he was in the aspect of a very changed activity, as mentioned above. And now he wants to draw close to Hashem, may He be blessed, to the One Who is Simple, and to be included among Israel, &ldquo;one nation in the land,&rdquo; through whom is the essential inclusion of all the diverse activities within the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut. For all the souls of Israel, the more they increase, the more they are included within the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut, as mentioned above.

And this is the great level of the convert who converts, about whom the Torah warned to draw him close and to love him, as it is written (Deuteronomy 10:19): &ldquo;And you shall love the convert.&rdquo; For this is very precious in the eyes of Hashem, may He be blessed, when the diverse activities are included within the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut. And the more the activity is changed and remote, when it merits to overcome and be included in the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut, the more precious it is. For this is His will, may He be blessed: that specifically through the very remote and changed activities, the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut should be revealed. This is the aspect of the great level of the ba&rsquo;al teshuvah [penitent] that our Sages of blessed memory spoke of: &ldquo;In the place where ba&rsquo;alay teshuvah stand, completely righteous people cannot stand&rdquo; (Berachos 34b). For because they were initially very far through their sins &mdash; which is the essential aspect of the diverse activities, for through each sin, G&ndash;d forbid, one distances oneself from there to the alma d&rsquo;feruda [world of separation], G&ndash;d forbid, which is the essential aspect of the very remote changed activities, as our Sages of blessed memory said &mdash; and therefore when one returns in teshuvah, the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut is revealed from within the very remote and changed activities, which is very precious in the eyes of Hashem, may He be blessed, as mentioned above.

And all the more so the convert, who was even more remote because he is not at all of the seed of Israel. Therefore when he comes to convert and is included in the holiness of Israel, &ldquo;one nation in the land,&rdquo; it is precious in the eyes of Hashem, may He be blessed, because the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut is revealed from the very remote changed activities, which is very precious in the eyes of Hashem, may He be blessed. And the more changed and remote the activities, the more precious it is in His eyes, may He be blessed, when the achdus ha&rsquo;pashut is revealed from there, as mentioned above.
